{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"f96dab42-28c1-480e-946a-f6cdbc33ec3d","title":"Derivative as the slope of the tangent line"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"f96dab42-28c1-480e-946a-f6cdbc33ec3d","step":{"id":"s1","script":"A derivative measures a curve’s slope at one point. Let’s find that slope for the parabola at x equals one.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Derivative = tangent slope"},{"op":"equation","id":"function","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"f(x)=x^2,\\qquad P=(1,1)"},{"op":"figure","id":"graph","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-0.3,2.4],"y":[-0.8,5.5],"axes":"edge","x_label":"x","y_label":"y"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"parabola","y":"x^2","domain":[-0.3,2.3]},{"kind":"point","id":"p","at":{"on":"parabola","x":1},"label":"P"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"f96dab42-28c1-480e-946a-f6cdbc33ec3d","step":{"id":"s2","script":"First use two points. The secant slope is the change in height divided by the change in x: three.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"secant_work","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"q","at":{"on":"parabola","x":2},"label":"Q (2, 4)"},{"kind":"segment","id":"secant","from":{"at":"p"},"to":{"at":"q"},"stroke":"dashed"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"secant_slope","anchor":{"el":"function","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"m_{\\text{secant}}=\\frac{f(2)-f(1)}{2-1}=3"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"f96dab42-28c1-480e-946a-f6cdbc33ec3d","step":{"id":"s3","script":"As the second point approaches P, secant slopes approach the tangent slope. That limiting slope is the derivative.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"tangent_work","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"near","at":{"on":"parabola","x":1.4}},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"p"},"to":{"at":"near"},"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"tangent","id":"tangent","to":"parabola","x":1,"length":1,"label":"tangent"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"limit","anchor":{"el":"secant_slope","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"f'(1)=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{(1+h)^2-1}{h}=\\lim_{h\\to0}(2+h)=2"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"f96dab42-28c1-480e-946a-f6cdbc33ec3d","step":{"id":"s4","script":"The tangent rises one unit for every half-unit across. Its slope is two, matching the derivative at P.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"slope_triangle","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"tangent_pt","at":[1.5,2]},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"p"},"to":[1.5,1],"stroke":"dashed","label":"0.5"},{"kind":"segment","from":[1.5,1],"to":{"at":"tangent_pt"},"stroke":"dashed","label":"1"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"result","anchor":{"el":"limit","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"m_{\\text{tangent}}=\\frac{1}{0.5}=2=f'(1)"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"What does f'(1) = 2 tell you about the curve at P?","expected_gist":"The tangent at P has slope 2."}}}
